# automation-moot-issue-regression-guard-pattern — History

## v1.1.0 (2026-06-30)

**Changed by:** ProjectHephaestus session investigating audit issue #1460 (strict
full-coverage audit citing a late `import re` inside `CIDriver._parse_json_block` at
`ci_driver.py:2125`).
**Verification:** verified-local (CI validation of the proposed regression test is PENDING)

### What changed (minor bump — new finding, same pattern)

- Added a new finding sibling to the original "moot issue / never-existed" case: the case
  where an audit finding is **ALREADY REMEDIATED by an earlier refactor that MOVED a symbol**
  into a shared helper — distinct from "the code never existed." The original v1.0.0 case is
  about premises that are wholly false; this v1.1.0 finding is about a premise that **was once
  true and has since been fixed**, with the cited **line number having drifted**.
- New core lesson: **re-locate the cited symbol by NAME (grep), never by the audit's
  `file:line`** — line numbers drift when the file changes after the audit snapshot. For #1460
  the cited `ci_driver.py:2125` had drifted to a different function (`_enable_auto_merge`);
  `_parse_json_block` had moved to line 2207, and the `import re` it allegedly contained was
  gone entirely — JSON parsing had been extracted into the shared helper
  `parse_json_block` in `hephaestus/automation/_review_utils.py` (extraction PR #1357), with
  `re` now imported correctly at module top-level in `_review_utils.py:36`. `ci_driver.py` no
  longer contained `import re` OR any `re.` usage.
- New lesson: **do NOT naively "move the import to the top"** when applying the literal
  remediation. Here that would have added an UNUSED `import re` to `ci_driver.py` and failed
  `ruff F401`. Verify the symbol is still REFERENCED before relocating its import; an audit
  finding can be obsolete.
- New regression-guard variant: a **static source-text assertion** test (read the module
  source, assert no in-body `import re`), mirroring the repo's existing static-analysis test
  style (`tests/unit/test_automation_boundary.py` greps source). This complements — does not
  replace — the AST-parse guard from v1.0.0; choose the source-text variant when the invariant
  is "no in-function/late import of X" rather than "exactly one definition of X."
- Added two Failed Attempts rows (trust-the-audit-line; apply-the-literal-import-move),
  a "When to Use" trigger, a v1.1.0 section, and tags
  (`already-remediated`, `line-number-drift`, `symbol-moved`, `ruff-f401`,
  `source-text-guard`, `verified-local`).
- Added `history:` frontmatter field. Bumped `version` 1.0.0 → 1.1.0. Date 2026-06-30.
- Verification of the v1.0.0 body remains `verified-ci`; the v1.1.0 additions are
  `verified-local` (grep/read evidence gathered directly from the source tree this session;
  no CI run validated the proposed regression test). The Overview table notes the pending CI
  validation honestly.

### Why

A strict full-coverage audit cites exact `file:line` coordinates that grow stale the moment the
file changes. Trusting the line blindly leads to editing the wrong function; applying the literal
fix when the finding is already remediated reintroduces dead code that fails lint. The durable
response is the same third path the v1.0.0 finding established — confirm the invariant is already
satisfied, then add a machine-checkable regression guard so the anti-pattern cannot silently
return — generalized here to the "symbol moved / line drifted / literal fix would reintroduce a
lint failure" shape, with a source-text guard variant.

### Risks / unverified assumptions carried forward

- Assumes `tests/unit/automation/test_ci_driver.py` already exists with a CIDriver construction
  fixture (`_make_ci_driver`) — NOT directly verified this session. The pure source-text guard
  test stands alone and does not depend on that fixture.
- Assumes ruff F401 is enforced on `ci_driver.py` (the stated reason not to add a top-level
  import) — inferred from repo lint config, not re-confirmed this session.
- The regression-guard test was NOT executed in CI — verification is local-evidence-only.

